d 'DUVM': module to estimate Deaton's unit-value model
d
d  duvm estimates the unit-value model of Deaton (1988, 1990, 1997
d ch. 5): own -and cross-price elasticities of quantity,
d expenditure elasticities and quality elasticities for a group of
d goods, from the budget shares and the unit values that households
d report, using the spatial variation of prices across clusters. It
d follows the book, in closed form and in Mata: a within-cluster
d first stage, a between-cluster second stage with the
d errors-in-variables correction, the quality correction through
d the separability parameter, the completion of the system with a
d composite good and an approximate Slutsky symmetry. Standard
d errors are linearized from the influence functions of both
d stages, by cluster or by survey design (svyset), or come from a
d design bootstrap of both stages. duvm also corrects the unit
d values for the selection of the buyers (Heckman, the probit in
d the standard errors), reports the elasticities of the household,
d of the individual or of the market, and makes the household size
d optional. duvmdiag and estat diagnostics check the price signal;
d predict and estat engel give the Engel curves; tables with
d significance stars can be written to Word, LaTeX, Excel, CSV or
d Markdown; a dialog box and help examples run from clickable links
d are included. Option compact reproduces the Stata code published
d with Deaton (1997). A technical note documents the model, the
d estimator and the validation of the variance; it is included as
d an ancillary file (duvm_technical_note.pdf). 
d
d KW: unit values
d KW: quality
d KW: price elasticities
d KW: demand
d KW: household survey
d KW: survey design
d KW: Deaton
d
d Requires: Stata version 14.2
d
d Distribution-Date: 20261003
d
d Author: Abdelkrim Araar,  Universite Laval 
d Support: email aabd@@ecn.ulaval.ca
d
f duvm.ado
